{"bill":{"id":"119-hr-3108","congress":119,"billType":"hr","billNumber":"3108","title":"RPM Access Act","introducedDate":"2025-04-30","latestActionDate":"2026-07-15","latestActionText":"Ordered to be Reported in the Nature of a Substitute by the Yeas and Nays: 39 - 0.","policyArea":"Health","subjects":["Congressional oversight","Health care costs and insurance","Health care coverage and access","Health care quality","Health technology, devices, supplies","Medicare"],"sponsorBioguideId":"K000392","cosponsorBioguideIds":["B001306","D000230","P000607","M001205","W000804","S001172","S001214","B001301","D000629"],"committeeCodes":[],"committeeNames":[],"officialSummary":"Rural Patient Monitoring Access Act or the RPM Access Act This bill conditions Medicare payment for remote patient monitoring services on certain requirements. Specifically, the bill conditions payment on (1) the ability of certain health care practitioners to be available in real time to respond to any detected anomalies; (2) the use of a system that can transmit relevant data in a format that is compatible with electronic health records, as needed; and (3) the reporting of such data, as required by the Centers for Medicare & Medicaid Services (CMS), to evaluate any cost savings as a result of such services. The bill also establishes a floor for certain payment calculations with respect to such services. The CMS must report on cost savings realized and expenses incurred from the use of such services over a four-year period.","congressDotGovUrl":"https://www.congress.gov/bill/119th-congress/house-bill/3108","source":"congress","sourceUrl":"https://www.govinfo.gov/bulkdata/BILLSTATUS/119/hr/BILLSTATUS-119hr3108.xml","fetchedAt":"2026-07-29T04:49:36.671Z"},"classification":{"billId":"119-hr-3108","plainSummary":"This bill sets new conditions for Medicare to pay for remote patient monitoring services. It requires health care practitioners to be available in real time to respond to problems detected by monitoring devices, mandates that monitoring systems transmit data in formats compatible with electronic health records, and requires reporting to help evaluate cost savings. The bill also establishes a minimum payment level for these services and directs the Centers for Medicare & Medicaid Services to report on costs and savings over four years.","industries":[{"industry":"health-providers","confidence":0.9,"rationale":"Hospitals and physician groups that offer remote monitoring services must meet new real-time availability and data reporting requirements to receive Medicare payment."},{"industry":"pharma","confidence":0.85,"rationale":"Medical device manufacturers that produce remote patient monitoring equipment face new technical and operational requirements for Medicare reimbursement."},{"industry":"tech","confidence":0.7,"rationale":"Software and IT service providers that develop remote monitoring platforms and electronic health record integration systems are affected by the compatibility and data transmission mandates."},{"industry":"insurance","confidence":0.5,"rationale":"Medicare Advantage plans and other insurers may see changes in reimbursement patterns and cost structures for remote monitoring services."}],"method":"llm","model":"claude-sonnet-4-5","inputHash":"b3b33c488705df1efc3eba4e","classifiedAt":"2026-07-30T02:30:05.397Z"},"plain":{"whatItDoes":"This bill would make Medicare payment for remote patient monitoring services depend on certain requirements.","whoItTouches":"People on Medicare. Anyone who buys health insurance.","everydayEffect":"If it passed, the change would show up in what Medicare pays for. Nothing has changed yet. Most bills never become law.","confidence":"crs-summary","source":"Reworded from the official summary. Staff at the Congressional Research Service wrote that summary. It is public domain, and the full text of it is further down this page.","titleInPlainWords":null},"overlaps":[{"billId":"119-hr-3108","bioguideId":"M001205","cyc